color-theme: genetics

=== layout: title-slide

# Lecture 05D

## Probability Concepts

## Chapter 5

## Dr. Neil Voss

## Sept 29, 2026

@notes

Flow: open with the hard pollen problem; expose the addition-rule trap; build the tools;
return to the pollen problem; finish with conditional inheritance. Aim for about 25 minutes,
including two short discussion pauses. These notes are cues, not a script. The closing Summary
section collects the two rule references and three study explanations for homework. Briefly
flag those resources in class, then skip ahead to the final takeaways when time is short.
Keep probabilities as fractions throughout. Read them aloud as shares of possible outcomes;
0 means impossible and 1 means certain. Counts and genotype ratios have different jobs.

=== layout: one-panel

# Learning objectives

@body

- Distinguish overlap from independence
- Choose [addition]{color=blue} or [multiplication]{color=orange}
- Predict offspring probabilities from gamete frequencies
- Interpret a conditional probability

@notes

Students should be able to justify a rule, not just recall its equation. The same three
examples anchor the lecture; a short cross makes "given" visible. Biology sets the probabilities.

=== layout: section

# Probability in crosses

@notes

Begin with a cross that the familiar 1:2:1 ratio cannot solve. Let students read the full
question on the next slide before introducing the probability rules.

=== layout: one-panel

# How do we solve this?

@body

Two Aa plants are crossed. Each produces equal numbers of A and a pollen and ovules.

However, A pollen is only half as likely as a pollen to succeed in fertilization.
Ovules are unaffected. Successful pollen fertilizes ovules at random, and all offspring
genotypes survive equally well.

What ratio of AA : Aa : aa offspring would you expect?

@notes

Opening challenge, adapted from the original pollen screenshot. Give students time to read
the complete problem. Success means surviving and participating in fertilization.
Ask: still 1:2:1? Which probabilities would you need? Take guesses without solving it yet.
Promise to return once we have tools for both a route to an offspring and alternative routes.

=== layout: two-panels

# What exactly are we counting?

@left

## [OR = at least one]{color=blue}

![B or C: either circle qualifies, including their shared region.](assets/lect05d-v2-probability_concepts/venn-union.png)

The union includes either event or both.

@right

## [AND = both]{color=orange}

![B and C: only the shared region qualifies.](assets/lect05d-v2-probability_concepts/venn-intersection.png)

The intersection contains outcomes in both events.

@notes

Use B and C for events so they are not confused with the A allele. An event is an outcome
or set of outcomes we care about. Ask what ordinary-language "or" might leave ambiguous;
our probability OR includes both. Neither keyword alone licenses a shortcut.
Use the recurring color cue: blue for addition/OR, orange for multiplication/AND.
Keep naming the operation and checking its condition; the color is a reminder, not the rule.
Point to the same three regions in both diagrams. The shaded region changes with the question.
These are schematic sets: their areas do not represent probabilities or establish independence.

=== layout: one-panel

# [Addition]{color=blue}

@body

## Can the events overlap?

@notes

Start where adding really works. Then change the biological context while keeping the
individual probabilities the same. That is the trap worth remembering.

=== layout: two-panels

# One gamete: A&#x2081; OR A&#x2082;?

@left

## Parent: A&#x2081;A&#x2082;

| Gamete | Probability |
| --- | --- |
| A&#x2081; | &#x00BD; |
| A&#x2082; | &#x00BD; |

@right

One allele at this locus

[P(A&#x2081; or A&#x2082;) = &#x00BD; + &#x00BD; = 1]{color=blue}

[Mutually exclusive = cannot both occur]{color=red}

@notes

Ask whether this ONE normal haploid gamete can carry both alleles at this locus. No.
Assume ordinary equal segregation. The two alternatives exhaust the possibilities here.
Mutually exclusive is also called disjoint. Keep "one gamete" explicit.

=== layout: two-panels

# [Rule card: addition]{color=blue}

@left

![Disjoint circles B and C have no shared outcomes.](assets/lect05d-v2-probability_concepts/venn-disjoint.png)

@right

## [OR: add alternatives]{color=blue}

[P(B or C) = P(B) + P(C)]{color=blue}

If [mutually exclusive]{color=red}:

Overlap: P(B and C) = 0

@notes

Point back to the single-gamete example. This is the special addition rule, and its
condition is part of the rule. Keep this card in mind for the next problem.
The separated circles show why there is no overlap to subtract. The regions are schematic;
their areas are not probability measurements.

=== layout: two-panels

# One offspring: A&#x2081; OR A&#x2083;?

@left

## A&#x2081;A&#x2082; &times; A&#x2083;A&#x2084;

These parents carry four alleles of one gene.

Each allele has probability &#x00BD; of being passed on.

Fertilization is random.

@right

B = inherits A&#x2081;

C = inherits A&#x2083;

[P(B or C) = ?]{color=blue}

Either allele, including both

@notes

This is the original four-allele screenshot rewritten as native text, with no textbook
exercise number. We now ask about one diploid offspring, not one haploid gamete.
Let students identify the two events before choosing an equation.

=== layout: two-panels

# So... every offspring qualifies?

@left

P(B) = &#x00BD;

P(C) = &#x00BD;

&#x00BD; + &#x00BD; = [1 ???]{color=red}

@right

## Vote: P(A&#x2081; or A&#x2083;)?

- A. 1 (every offspring)
- B. &#x00BE;
- C. &#x00BD;
- D. &#x00BC;

@notes

Present the tempting answer as a claim to test, not an answer to memorize. Vote before
the square: B is correct. The arithmetic is fine; the untested condition is the problem.
Ask whether one offspring could inherit both. Do not repair the claim yet.

=== layout: two-panels

# Does every offspring qualify?

@left

|  | A&#x2083; | A&#x2084; |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A&#x2081; | ? | ? |
| A&#x2082; | ? | ? |

@right

## Discuss

- Fill the four offspring
- Mark A&#x2081; OR A&#x2083;
- Find a counterexample

Think: 30 sec; pair: 1 min

@notes

Pairs or Zoom chat: fill the square, then decide which cells qualify. Expected counterexample:
A2A4. Each cell has probability &#x00BC; under the stated assumptions. Ask for a reason before
advancing; this is the main addition-rule misconception checkpoint.

=== layout: two-panels

# Three outcomes qualify

@left

|  | A&#x2083; | A&#x2084; |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A&#x2081; | [A&#x2081;A&#x2083;: BOTH]{color=orange} | A&#x2081;A&#x2084;: YES |
| A&#x2082; | A&#x2082;A&#x2083;: YES | [A&#x2082;A&#x2084;: NO]{color=red} |

@right

P(A&#x2081; or A&#x2083;) = [&#x00BE;]{color=blue}

Three of four equally likely outcomes qualify.

Where did we count twice?

@notes

Keep the same square orientation throughout this sequence. Read the YES/NO/BOTH labels,
not just the colors. The shared A1A3 cell is the clue for repairing the rule.

=== layout: two-panels

# One offspring, counted twice

@left

|  | A&#x2083; | A&#x2084; |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A&#x2081; | [B and C]{color=orange} | B only |
| A&#x2082; | C only | Neither |

@right

B: top row &rarr; &#x00BD;

C: left column &rarr; &#x00BD;

[Shared cell: subtract one &#x00BC;]{color=red}

@notes

Trace the top row and then the left column. The shared cell is visited twice.
We subtract one copy, not the whole outcome: inclusive OR must still include it once.

=== layout: stacked-panels

# [Rule card: addition, with overlap]{color=blue}

@top

![Adding B and C counts the shared region twice; subtract one copy.](assets/lect05d-v2-probability_concepts/venn-double-count.png)

@bottom

[P(B or C) = P(B) + P(C) &minus; P(B and C)]{color=blue}

Mutually exclusive? The last term is zero.

@notes

This general rule works whether events overlap or not. The earlier special rule is
the zero-overlap case. The square gives the equation a meaning before we name it.
Point to the overlap and connect it to the shared B-and-C cell in the preceding square.
The diagram labels count visits to a region, not offspring or probability values.

=== layout: two-panels

# Callback: correct the double count

@left

|  | A&#x2083; | A&#x2084; |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A&#x2081; | [Both: &#x00BC;]{color=orange} | B only: &#x00BC; |
| A&#x2082; | C only: &#x00BC; | Neither: &#x00BC; |

@right

## [General addition rule]{color=blue}

[P(B or C) = &#x00BD; + &#x00BD; &minus; &#x00BC;]{color=blue}

= [&#x00BE;]{color=blue}

Count each cell once

@notes

Connect the formula to the same square. An alternative check is 1 minus the probability
of the neither cell. Keep the focus on why the subtraction is necessary.

=== layout: two-panels

# Same probabilities, different context

@left

## ONE gamete

A&#x2081; OR A&#x2082;

Cannot carry both

&#x00BD; + &#x00BD; = [1]{color=blue}

@right

## ONE offspring

A&#x2081; OR A&#x2083;

Can inherit both

&#x00BD; + &#x00BD; &minus; &#x00BC; = [&#x00BE;]{color=blue}

@notes

Addition takeaway: name the event, identify the unit being sampled, and check overlap.
Same numbers do not guarantee the same answer. Now ask how to calculate an AND probability
when drawing every outcome becomes inconvenient.

=== layout: one-panel

# [Multiplication]{color=orange}

@body

## Does one event change the other probability?

@notes

Use the familiar square first. Then return to the hard pollen problem with the same
method but unequal gamete probabilities. Independence is a condition, not a synonym for equal chances.

=== layout: two-panels

# One offspring: A&#x2081; AND A&#x2083;

@left

|  | A&#x2083;: &#x00BD; | A&#x2084;: &#x00BD; |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A&#x2081;: &#x00BD; | [BOTH: &#x00BC;]{color=orange} | &#x00BC; |
| A&#x2082;: &#x00BD; | &#x00BC; | &#x00BC; |

@right

&#x00BD; of the offspring inherit A&#x2081;

Of those, &#x00BD; also inherit A&#x2083;

[&#x00BD; &times; &#x00BD; = &#x00BC;]{color=orange}

@notes

Ask students to point to the AND cell before reading the calculation. Random union
means the allele from one parent does not change the allele probabilities from the other.
Multiplication is taking a fraction of a fraction.

=== layout: two-panels

# [Rule card: multiplication]{color=orange}

@left

![The intersection of B and C contains the outcomes where both occur.](assets/lect05d-v2-probability_concepts/venn-intersection.png)

@right

## [AND: both events]{color=orange}

[P(B and C) = P(B) &times; P(C)]{color=orange}

For [independent events]{color=red}:

Knowing that one event occurred does not change the probability of the other.

@notes

This is the special multiplication rule. Independent events may occur together;
our A1 and A3 events do. We will revisit the distinction from mutually exclusive.
An overlap means both events can happen. It does not by itself establish independence;
that condition comes from the biological model, not from the circles' shapes or areas.

=== layout: two-panels

# Back to our hard question

@left

## Plants: Aa &times; Aa

Pollen success: A:a = 1:2

Ovules: unaffected

F&#x2082; ratio: AA : Aa : aa = ?

@right

## Successful pollen

Vote: P(A) = ?

- A. &#x00BC;
- B. &#x2153;
- C. &#x00BD;
- D. &#x2154;

@notes

Repeat the opening problem before solving it. Vote: B is correct. Equal production does
not imply equal contribution to offspring. We need frequencies among successful pollen,
not all pollen produced. A is an unnormalized weight; C ignores selection; D is the a
frequency. Explain these distractors after the next slide, not before the vote.

=== layout: one-panel

# Example: count successful pollen

@body

| Pollen allele | Produced | Successful |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A | 100 | [50]{color=green} |
| a | 100 | [100]{color=green} |

@notes

These illustrative counts satisfy the stated relative success; absolute survival was not given.
Other survival rates with the same ratio give the same allele frequencies. Ask: among successful
pollen, what belongs in the denominator: 100, 150, or 200? Count before writing a formula.

=== layout: two-panels

# Which pollen can fertilize?

@left

## Successful pool

[50 A + 100 a = 150 total]{color=blue}

A share: 50 &divide; 150 = [&#x2153;]{color=green}

a share: 100 &divide; 150 = [&#x2154;]{color=green}

@right

## Among successful pollen

A : a = 1 : 2

Shares sum to 1

@notes

The denominator is 150 successful pollen, not 200 produced. This answers the vote: B, &#x2153;.
The same reasoning with weights gives (&#x00BD; times &#x00BD;) divided by (&#x00BC; + &#x00BD;).
Relative success of &#x00BD; does not mean a final allele frequency of &#x00BC;.

=== layout: two-panels

# One genotype, two gamete pools

@left

## Ovules

| Allele | Probability |
| --- | --- |
| A | &#x00BD; |
| a | &#x00BD; |

@right

## Successful pollen

| Allele | Probability |
| --- | --- |
| A | [&#x2153;]{color=green} |
| a | [&#x2154;]{color=green} |

@notes

Compare the allele frequencies transmitted through ovules and successful pollen.
The ovule side stays equal; the pollen side reflects unequal success in fertilization.
Assume random union of these gametes: independence does not require equal frequencies.

=== layout: two-panels

# Your turn: weight the square

@left

## Successful pollen

| Ovule | A: &#x2153; | a: &#x2154; |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A: &#x00BD; | AA: &#x2159; | Aa: ? |
| a: &#x00BD; | Aa: ? | aa: ? |

@right

## Predict

[AA: (&#x00BD;) &times; (&#x2153;) = &#x2159;]{color=orange}

Fill the other three cells

30 sec; compare answers

@notes

Callback to the multiplication card: multiply along a route. Give the AA cell as a
worked example, then pause. Expected remaining values: top right &#x2153;, bottom left &#x2159;,
bottom right &#x2153;. Ask whether four cells still means four equally likely outcomes.

=== layout: two-panels

# Four cells, unequal probabilities

@left

## Successful pollen

| Ovule | A: &#x2153; | a: &#x2154; |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A: &#x00BD; | AA: &#x2159; | [Aa: &#x2153;]{color=blue} |
| a: &#x00BD; | [Aa: &#x2159;]{color=blue} | aa: &#x2153; |

@right

## Check

[&#x2159; + &#x2153; + &#x2159; + &#x2153; = 1]{color=blue}

Two routes to Aa

Can ONE offspring take BOTH routes?

@notes

The cells total 1. The Aa cells differ in which parent supplied A. For one offspring,
those routes are mutually exclusive. This is the place where the special addition rule
really does apply, even though the resulting genotype is the same.

=== layout: two-panels

# Aa: [multiply within]{color=orange}, [add between]{color=blue}

@left

## [Ovule A AND pollen a]{color=orange}

[(&#x00BD;) &times; (&#x2154;) = &#x2153;]{color=orange}

[OR]{color=blue}

[Ovule a AND pollen A]{color=orange}

[(&#x00BD;) &times; (&#x2153;) = &#x2159;]{color=orange}

@right

## Mutually exclusive routes

[P(Aa) = &#x2153; + &#x2159;]{color=blue}

= [&#x00BD;]{color=blue}

@notes

Physically trace across a route for multiplication and then between routes for addition.
Same genotype, different parental origins. The condition for adding is about the events,
not about whether their genotype labels look alike.

=== layout: two-panels

# Our opening problem, solved

@left

| Type | P | Ratio |
| --- | --- | --- |
| AA | &#x2159; | 1 |
| Aa | &#x00BD; | 3 |
| aa | &#x2153; | 2 |

@right

## [AA : Aa : aa = 1 : 3 : 2]{color=green}

A pollen contributes less

Two routes produce Aa

@notes

Return explicitly to the guesses on the opening slide. We did not need a new probability
rule: we needed the right gamete pool and the old rules in the right places. Ratios are
expected proportions, not a guarantee that every six offspring fit the ratio exactly.

=== layout: two-panels

# Callback: two rules, two jobs

@left

## [AND: within a route]{color=orange}

[Ovule A AND pollen a]{color=orange}

[&#x00BD; &times; &#x2154; = &#x2153;]{color=orange}

Condition: random union

@right

## [OR: between routes]{color=blue}

Two exclusive routes to Aa

[&#x2153; + &#x2159; = &#x00BD;]{color=blue}

Condition: no overlap

@notes

Section takeaway: multiply within a route, add distinct routes, and state the conditions.
What if learning one event happened changes the other probability? That leads to the
general multiplication rule, using a conditional probability.

=== layout: section

# Conditional probability

## What changes when we know more?

@notes

Shift from changing the successful gamete pool to changing the information available.
First make the restricted group visible in a familiar cross, then use the family model.

=== layout: two-panels

# Given: this offspring is not aa

@left

## Aa &times; Aa

| Gamete | A | a |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A | AA | Aa |
| a | Aa | aa |

@right

## Vote: P(Aa given not aa)?

- A. 0
- B. &#x00BD;
- C. &#x2154;
- D. 1

@notes

Use equal segregation and random fertilization; all four starting cells are equally likely.
Ask which cell the new information rules out. Do not give a formula yet. Correct answer: C.
Ask students choosing one-half: are you counting all offspring or only those that are not aa?

=== layout: two-panels

# Keep only the possible outcomes

@left

| Outcome | Given not aa |
| --- | --- |
| AA | Keep |
| [Aa]{color=green} | Keep |
| [Aa]{color=green} | Keep |
| aa | [Exclude]{color=red} |

@right

## Two of three are Aa

P(Aa given not aa) = [&#x2154;]{color=green}

Before: &#x00BD;. Given not aa: &#x2154;.

[Condition sets the group]{color=green}

@notes

The two Aa rows are distinct gamete combinations, not a duplicated count. Among the three
equally likely remaining routes, two give Aa. The information changes our reference group,
not the offspring's genotype. Keep this count-first meaning of "given" for the family example.

=== layout: two-panels

# A family inheritance question

@left

## Huntington disease model

H = dominant disease allele

Affected father: Hh

Mother: hh

@right

## Their young adult son

Genotype unknown; no symptoms yet

Child's other parent: hh

Who could inherit H?

@notes

Adapt the third textbook screenshot without its exercise number. In this simplified
late-onset model, an H carrier will eventually develop disease; absence of symptoms at
this young age does not distinguish Hh from hh. Stipulate Hh for the affected father,
hh for both unrelated parents, and no new mutation. These assumptions define the exercise.
The next two questions preserve its original parts: the son's risk, then his child's.

=== layout: stacked-panels

# First: the son's probability?

@top

P(son inherits H) = ?

No symptoms yet: useful evidence?

@bottom

| Mother's egg | Father's sperm: H | Father's sperm: h |
| --- | --- | --- |
| h | ? | ? |

@notes

Pause for an answer before filling the two possible outcomes. The hh mother contributes
h to every child. Under our stated age assumption, lack of current symptoms does not
change the inheritance probability. Avoid treating symptom absence as a genotype test.

=== layout: stacked-panels

# The son: two possible genotypes

@top

P(son inherits H) = [&#x00BD;]{color=green}

Same eventual disease risk, under our model

@bottom

| Mother's egg | Father's sperm: H | Father's sperm: h |
| --- | --- | --- |
| h: 1 | [Hh: &#x00BD;]{color=green} | hh: &#x00BD; |

@notes

This answers the first part of the source question. Hh and hh are the only possibilities.
Keep inheritance and eventual symptoms distinct concepts, even though our simplified
model gives them the same probability. Now follow the allele for another generation.

=== layout: two-panels

# Next: his child's probability?

@left

## Son's genotype: unknown

Hh with probability &#x00BD;

hh with probability &#x00BD;

Child's other parent: hh

@right

## Vote: P(child inherits H)?

- A. 0
- B. &#x00BC;
- C. &#x00BD;
- D. 1

@notes

Vote: B is correct. The child has a &#x00BD; transmission probability only IF the son has H.
An hh son cannot transmit H in this model. Ask whether &#x00BD; applies on both branches.
Distinguish not knowing the son's genotype from knowing he is Hh.

=== layout: one-panel

# Follow both possible routes

@body

| Son's genotype | Probability | P(child H given genotype) | Route probability |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Hh | &#x00BD; | &#x00BD; | [&#x00BC;]{color=orange} |
| hh | &#x00BD; | 0 | 0 |
| Either | 1 |  | [Total: &#x00BC;]{color=blue} |

@notes

Define S as the son has H and C as his child has H. Multiply within each route:
P(S and C) = (&#x00BD;)(&#x00BD;) = &#x00BC;. Add the mutually exclusive routes: P(C) = &#x00BC; + 0 = &#x00BC;.
This answers the second part. P(C given S) is &#x00BD;, whereas P(C) is &#x00BC;.
The table uses native editable text; there is no screenshot to decode.

=== layout: one-panel

# [Rule card: general multiplication]{color=orange}

@body

S = son has H; C = child has H

[P(S and C) = P(S) &times; P(C given S)]{color=orange}

P(C given S) = P(C | S)

Conditional = restricted to a known event

Independent? P(C given S) = P(C)

@notes

The vertical bar means "given," not OR or division. Here S is the son has H and C is
the child has H. These events are dependent: knowing S changes C from &#x00BC; to &#x00BD;.
The general rule works for independent events too, reducing to the earlier rule card.

=== layout: two-panels

# Can we reverse "given"?

@left

## Parent &rarr; child

P(child H given son H) = &#x00BD;

Known: the son has H

@right

## Vote: child &rarr; parent

P(son H given child H) = ?

- A. 0
- B. &#x00BC;
- C. &#x00BD;
- D. 1

@notes

Vote: D is correct. Restate that the child's other parent is hh and we assume no new mutation.
Where could the child's H have come from? This is a reversal of information, not a
reversal of biological inheritance. Expected answer: 1, not &#x00BD;.

=== layout: two-panels

# The evidence identifies the route

@left

| Possible source of H | Fits the model? |
| --- | --- |
| The son | YES |
| The other parent: hh | NO |
| New mutation | Excluded |

@right

P(son H given child H) = [1]{color=green}

P(child H given son H) = &#x00BD;

[Direction matters]{color=red}

@notes

Correct the reversed conditional in the old deck: with these assumptions, a child who
has inherited H establishes that the son has H. This does not make the unconditional
probability for the child equal to 1; that remains &#x00BC; before observing the child's genotype.

=== layout: two-panels

# Bayes: keep only the known group

@left

## We know: the child has H

P(son H and child H) = &#x00BC;

P(child H) = &#x00BC;

P(son H given child H) = [1]{color=green}

@right

## Divide by P(C)

P(S | C) = P(S and C) / P(C)

= [P(C | S) &times; P(S)]{color=orange} / P(C)

Here: (&#x00BD; &times; &#x00BD;) / (&#x00BC;)

@notes

S means the son has H; C means the child has H. The denominator must be nonzero.
All of the probability mass in the child-H group belongs to the son-H route, so the
conditional fraction is 1. Name Bayes' rule after this reasoning. No extra formula image
or memorization exercise is needed. The expanded denominator was already built in the route table.

=== layout: two-panels

# Independent is not mutually exclusive

@left

## One gamete: A&#x2081; / A&#x2082;

Cannot carry both

Mutually exclusive: YES

Independent: NO

Knowing A&#x2081; rules out A&#x2082;

@right

## One offspring: A&#x2081; / A&#x2083;

Can inherit both

Mutually exclusive: NO

Independent: YES

Knowing A&#x2081; leaves P(A&#x2083;) = &#x00BD;

@notes

Return to the four-allele cross and its random-fertilization assumption. Compare the
same two questions: can both happen, and does knowing one change the other probability?
For the gamete, P(A2) changes from &#x00BD; to zero after learning A1. For the offspring,
P(A3) stays &#x00BD; after learning A1. Ask students to explain one side in their own words.

=== layout: two-panels

# Three aa offspring. What comes next?

@left

## Known parents: Aa &times; Aa

| Offspring | Genotype |
| --- | --- |
| First | aa |
| Second | aa |
| Third | aa |
| Next | ? |

@right

## Vote: P(next is aa)?

- A. 0
- B. &#x00BC;
- C. &#x00BE;
- D. 1

@notes

Return to the ordinary Aa x Aa model: equal segregation, random fertilization, and independent
offspring outcomes. Both parental genotypes are known; this is not the unequal-pollen model.
Ask students to vote before revealing the square. B is correct. Invite an explanation from
someone who thinks the cross must now balance out, or that a run of aa must continue.
This applies the textbook's warning that previous offspring do not set the next outcome.

=== layout: two-panels

# Each offspring gets a fresh draw

@left

## Same Aa &times; Aa cross

| Gamete | A | a |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A | AA | Aa |
| a | Aa | [aa]{color=orange} |

@right

## Still [&#x00BC;]{color=orange}

One aa route out of four

Earlier offspring: no change

Recall: [independence]{color=orange}

[Ratios are not quotas]{color=red}

@notes

The four cells are possible gamete combinations for each offspring, not four slots to fill
with successive offspring. Earlier aa offspring do not use up the aa cell. Each parent's
A and a probabilities remain one-half, so the multiplication rule still gives one-quarter.
Compare with "given this offspring is not aa": that information removed a possibility for
the offspring being considered. Knowing a sibling is aa removes none of this offspring's cells
under our fixed-parent, independent-trials model. If parental genotypes were unknown, offspring
could instead provide evidence about them; do not generalize this result to that situation.
Ask what condition made the shortcut valid, then move to the exit check.

=== layout: one-panel

# Exit check: name the condition

@body

| Familiar problem | Which rule, and why? |
| --- | --- |
| A&#x2081; OR A&#x2083; in one offspring | ? |
| Two gamete routes to Aa | ? |
| Child H, given son H | ? |

@notes

Give 30 seconds to name a rule and its condition, not just a number. Ask students to
choose one row to explain aloud or in chat. This checks transfer between our examples
without introducing another story at the end of the lecture.

=== layout: one-panel

# The condition chooses the rule

@body

| Problem | Reasoning | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A&#x2081; OR A&#x2083; | [Add; subtract overlap]{color=blue} | &#x00BE; |
| Two routes to Aa | [Multiply within]{color=orange}; [add between]{color=blue} | &#x00BD; |
| Child inherits H | Given an Hh parent | &#x00BD; |

@notes

For row two, we mean the unequal-pollen example: &#x2153; plus &#x2159;. For row three, compare
the conditional &#x00BD; with the unconditional child probability of &#x00BC;. If students give
only an operator, ask what makes that operator valid here.

=== layout: section

# Summary

## Rules and worked explanations for homework

@notes

These are the key references to return to while doing homework. Repeating the rules here
signals their importance and keeps both forms of each rule together on one slide.
Point students to this section, then choose how much to review aloud.

=== layout: one-panel

# [Addition Rules]{color=blue}

@body

Use OR when either event qualifies, including outcomes where both occur.

*Mutually exclusive events*

If B and C cannot occur together, there is no overlap:

[P(B or C) = P(B) + P(C)]{color=blue}

*General rule: with or without overlap*

[P(B or C) = P(B) + P(C) &minus; P(B and C)]{color=blue}

Subtract the shared outcomes once because the sum counted them twice.
For mutually exclusive events, P(B and C) = 0.

@notes

Single-slide homework reference, repeating the earlier addition rule cards in one place.
Connect the overlap term to the shared Venn region and the A1A3 cell in the Punnett square.
The general rule always works; the first equation is its zero-overlap special case.

=== layout: one-panel

# [Multiplication Rules]{color=orange}

@body

Use AND when both events must occur.

*Independent events*

Knowing B occurred does not change the probability of C:

[P(B and C) = P(B) &times; P(C)]{color=orange}

*General rule: with or without independence*

[P(B and C) = P(B) &times; P(C | B)]{color=orange}

Equivalently: [P(B and C) = P(C) &times; P(B | C)]{color=orange}

P(C | B) means the probability of C given B. For independent events, P(C | B) = P(C).

@notes

Single-slide homework reference, repeating the earlier multiplication rule cards in one place.
The bar means "given." Either order works when the conditional probability matches that order.
Recall the family example: a child's probability changes when we learn the parent's genotype.

=== layout: one-panel

# Study reference: choosing a rule

@body

A probability describes a share of possible outcomes. Here, &#x00BC; means one of four
equally likely outcomes, while 0 means impossible and 1 means certain. A probability
does not promise a particular result in a small family.

Use the [addition rule]{color=blue} when either event qualifies. If both events can occur
in the same offspring, adding their probabilities counts that offspring twice.
Subtract the overlap once so that each qualifying outcome is counted once.

Use the [multiplication rule]{color=orange} when both events must occur. Multiply the
probability of the first event by the probability of the second given the first.
For independent events, knowing the first occurred does not change the second probability.

@notes

Study reference: skip during the live lecture unless students request a recap. These paragraphs
make the rule conditions available in the exported slides, without requiring access to notes.

=== layout: one-panel

# Study reference: the pollen cross

@body

Equal pollen production does not mean equal contribution to offspring. If 50 A pollen and
100 a pollen succeed, the successful pool contains 150 pollen. Its allele probabilities
are 50 &divide; 150 = &#x2153; for A and 100 &divide; 150 = &#x2154; for a.
The ovule probabilities stay &#x00BD; for A and &#x00BD; for a.

Within each cell, [multiply]{color=orange} the ovule and pollen probabilities.

For AA: &#x00BD; &times; &#x2153; = &#x2159;. For aa: &#x00BD; &times; &#x2154; = &#x2153;.

Aa has two routes. [Add]{color=blue} their probabilities: &#x2153; + &#x2159; = &#x00BD;.
Thus AA, Aa, and aa have probabilities
&#x2159;, &#x00BD;, and &#x2153;. These are one, three, and two parts out of six,
giving the genotype ratio 1:3:2.

@notes

Study reference: skip during class. The counts are an illustration of relative pollen success,
not measured data. Fractions describe probabilities; the ratio compares genotype proportions.

=== layout: one-panel

# Study reference: what "given" changes

@body

"Given" tells us which outcomes are still possible. In an Aa &times; Aa cross, knowing
that an offspring is not aa leaves three equally likely cells: AA, Aa, and Aa.
Two of those three cells are Aa, so the probability is &#x2154;.

In the family example, the son has probability &#x00BD; of carrying H. If he carries H,
he passes it to his child with probability &#x00BD;. Before we know his genotype, the
child's probability is &#x00BD; &times; &#x00BD; = &#x00BC;. Given that the son carries H,
the child's probability is instead &#x00BD;.

The direction matters. Given that the child inherited H, the son must carry H under
our model: the other parent is hh, and there is no new mutation. That probability is 1.
We are now asking about the son, using information about the child.

@notes

Study reference: skip during class, then show the summary. Conditional probabilities answer
different questions; changing the known information changes the group being considered.

=== layout: one-panel

# Summary: context before calculation

@body

- Define the event and the population
- [OR: add; subtract shared outcomes]{color=blue}
- [AND: multiply; check independence]{color=orange}
- Given: restrict to the known group
- Check totals against the biology

@notes

Close the loop: the opening pollen puzzle needed successful-pollen frequencies, multiplication
within routes, and addition between routes. The four-allele trap exposed overlap; the family
example exposed direction in conditional probabilities. Ask: which condition will you check
before using each rule? Leave this summary visible as the deck ends.

=== layout: theend

# THE END

@notes

Pause for final questions before closing this deck.
